welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Lawrence Liang a legal researcher and  lawyer based in Bangalore India known  for his legal campaigns on issues of  public concern he is here at Yale as the  rice visiting fellow in the South Asian  Studies counsel at the Macmillan Center  his key areas of interest are law  popular culture and piracy professor  Liang has been working closely with the  suraíh program at the Center for the  Study of developing societies in Delhi  on a joint research project on  intellectual property today we talk with  him about the recent ruling on the Delhi  University photocopy case thanks my  pleasure to be here so let's begin with  an overview of the photocopy case tell  us about it well what happened was  around four years ago some of the  leading academic publishers Oxford  Cambridge Francis and Taylor filed a  copyright infringement case against a  small photocopying shop at Delhi  University along with Delhi University  and this was about course backs so  typically you know for the graduate  level a lot of the courses are designed  using extracts from different books and  the publishers claimed that this was a  violation of copyright we filed an  intervention on behalf of the students  and on behalf of the teachers arguing  that this was actually legally  permissible in the law and I think the  importance of the issue was that this  was the test case both for India but  also for other developing countries on  what the extent of the fair use  exception in copyright law would be to  facilitate access to knowledge in a  situation where learning materials are  extremely expensive and in a country as  sharply divided as India is this can  mean a great impact on the ability for  students to access learning what do you  think there's a reason the publishers  chose this particular photocopy shop in  the India versus you know something in  part of the world for instance well  absolutely I mean number of reasons for  that one will that the photocopy shop is  located within the university they had a  contract with the University and they  were in fact given library cards which  will allow them to take books before the  start of term to create these course  backs the second is that in a way that  the efficiency of the photocopying shop  where it became the most popular and to  go to place for people and for students  to access material you should remember  that in many libraries in India you have  either a single copy of a book or in  many instances you don't even have a  copy of the book  so come exam time a lot of students rely  on being able to get these books through  photocopy shop like this one okay so  what was the crux of the case the crux  of the case was that there is an  exception in copyright law in India  which allows for the reproduction of the  work for educational purposes and it  literally says reproduction of the work  in the course of instruction the  publishers wanted a narrow reading of  this to mean literally giving a copy to  a student in class and taking it back  from them whereas we were we were  arguing that obviously that cannot be  the interpretation and it had to be on a  much wider terrain the second big issue  was the question of how much you could  copy so as you know in the u.s. in  overall series of cases over the decades  they've laid down a quantitative  restriction of 10% of a book being  permissible for education that's  interesting I did not know that actually  I would have said it was much greater  than that right so that's it in fact  precise question the question is that  for a country like us where the  infrastructure of education is already  very well established if you have a rule  of 10% then the question is would that  be adequate for a country like India  should you have a differential standard  for the developing countries on  education and that's why it's an  important case for the rest of the world  because in a way the globalization of  intellectual property and it is the  global regime has also been accompanied  in a way by the use of standards from  cases primarily from the US and it's  very rare that you actually have cases  from developing countries that lay out  what kind of different philosophical  premised on how we understand copyright  law okay what was I believe it was  justice and laws what was his actual  so just and lo concluded with the claim  that there was no prima facie  infringement claim that had been made  the reason being that if copyright is a  right that's granted by statute it's  also accompanied by exceptions copyright  you must remember began as a law that  wanted to facilitate public access so  the very first copyright statute the  statute of Anne is entitled a law for  the Advancement of learning but over the  decades copyright has primarily become  about the protection of the rights of  private interests and about the rights  in a way of the owners of copyright so I  think it's in the in the manner of  maintaining a balance between public  interests and access to learning  material and promoting creativity while  at the same time ensuring that there are  some incentives for creators of works so  what just landlord did was that he  bought brought this balance together to  say that in the case of Education there  is a policy kind of a you know a premise  which informs why there is an exception  carved out for education and that does  not interfere with the legitimate  interests of the publishers and if that  is the case then they can be no claim  that there was an infringement for  making of course backs okay and do you  think that okay was this solely his his  argument based solely on educational  institutions for instance if I'm a  private citizen and I want to photocopy  you know a book and give it to my sister  for instance well will that fall under  this ruling or no so one major  difference between the US and India in  terms of the structure of copyright law  is that in the u.s. you don't have  enumerated exceptions what do you have a  general principle so there are four  principles that would determine whether  the use of a work is fair or not but in  the Indian context you actually have  enumerated exceptions so there are other  exceptions for private use but that's  primarily for research so if you wanted  to let Li photocopy JK Rowling's Harry  Potter books and give it to someone that  would not fall within fairness the  provision that we were arguing for was  specifically about educational uses so  the question is assuming I want to do in  an English literature course photocopy  two chapters of Rowling's Harry Potter  would that be allowed  the answer was absolutely okay and you  say we did you actually work on this  case yourself yes I mean we were part of  a team that can that consisted of a  number of lawyers advising the students  and teachers even though they were not a  party's with the case our argument was  that this is a case that's about the  future of Education and it cannot be  fought without the voice of students and  teachers being heard one of the ironies  of the cases that the petitioners the  publishers cited the use of textbooks  and academic books by a number of  academics these academics signed on on  the case to say that we do not have a  problem with students using our material  for courses and what do you think the  implications of this ruling will be not  only for India now but also more  globally I think one of the key  challenges in terms of global copyright  and the access to education movement has  been the question of how do you balance  between on the one hand your obligations  under international law because all  countries are a signatory to the TRIPS  Agreement which lays out a common  minimum standard in copyright but at the  same time because the needs of every  country is unique and the developmental  challenges are extremely in a way  individual how do you balance between  your legal obligation versus the  flexibilities that you can use as a  country and I think what this case  leaves down is a very good precedent for  other countries to follow especially  from a developing countries work  perspective on how you can interpret  your laws even while remaining compliant  with international standards okay and in  terms of the publishers themselves I'm  not familiar actually with the legal  system in India are can the publishers  appeal or no well unfortunately the  publishers have already opinion the urge  them once the decision came out a number  of intellectuals including you know the  Nobel Prize when amar TSN when the case  had been you know filed had written to  them as as authors with Oxford urging  the publishers not to you know take this  kind of agnostic stance over the course  of the last four years in a way they've  already  a number of students a number of  academics and so when the judgment came  out a number of us who had written after  the judgement urged the publishers not  to appeal and we would try to make the  case that students and teachers are not  your greatest enemies they're your  greatest allies so you have to think in  a way of how you were to you know create  and think about a business model that  ensures that it's feasible for you even  while at the same time not impeding  access to knowledge  unfortunately the publishers haven't  heeded our call and they filed in the  field so we will have to take this all  the way to the Supreme Court okay do you  think that these publishers have you  heard of any instances of these  publishers starting the same call it  trouble and other developing parts of  the world I think it's important here to  remember that the Indian market is a  particularly lucrative one both in terms  of the size of the student but also  because it's the the primary language of  Education at the university level is  English so in a way this is one of the  most important markets for the academic  publishers which is I think why they  wanted to use this as a test-case this  is of course happened in developed  countries and in the u.s. this has been  happening for many many decades but in  terms of the developing country this is  one of the first major cases okay and in  terms of the appeal what do you think  the outcome will be  well we're hopeful because the judgement  is a very sound one and it's kind of  very solid in its interpretation of the  law they'll be hopeful that this will be  you know kind of it will not be  overturned on appeal how long is the has  the process been in terms of you know  the beginning of the case and then how  long will it the appeal take the case  was initiated four years ago so in  between the initiation of the case to  the judgment at the high court level  it's been four years they've appealed  now to what is known as a division bench  which is a larger bench within the High  Court and assuming it goes our way then  they may appeal to the Supreme Court and  vice versa  if it goes against us but in a way this  is a part of a much larger movement  that's been taking place for the last 20  years and Yale has had a very important  role to play in that because the Yale  Law  school which is very active on debates  on intellectual property from a global  perspective has been crucial and  anchoring an access to knowledge  movement so I think that this is seen  and followed by the global community of  people working on intellectual property  from a public interest perspective so  it's I think it's wonderful that you've  kind of almost come full circle with the  L having started it and you're working  on this case and in the end being here  with us today no it's absolutely for  teachers when we hope that this  collaboration will continue because this  debate is not going to end with this  case right okay thank you very much for  being here with us today and sharing  some of your work thanks Matt for more  information about professor Liang and  his research please visit our website at  macmillan report yale.edu  be sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at you
 

